\section{Evaluation}
\label{sec:evaluation}

Our comprehensive evaluation demonstrates the effectiveness and limitations of LLM-generated synthetic spam data through systematic analysis of 2,040 experimental configurations spanning two complementary tracks. We evaluate two state-of-the-art LLM approaches (GPT-4.1-mini and Claude-3.5-Haiku) through forward detection experiments (66 configurations, 1,320 training-evaluation units) assessing whether synthetic data can effectively train spam classifiers, and reverse detection experiments (36 configurations, 720 training-evaluation units) evaluating whether classifiers can detect AI-powered spam from different LLM sources, all replicated across 20 independent groups. The results reveal critical dependencies on model selection, prompt engineering, synthetic data proportion, and cross-model transfer characteristics. Through rigorous statistical analysis incorporating confidence intervals, hypothesis testing, and sensitivity metrics, we establish quantitative relationships between LLM generation quality and practical utility for both spam detection training and AI-spam detection.

\subsection{Forward Detection: LLM-Generated Data for Spam Classifier Training}

The forward detection experiments evaluate whether LLM-generated synthetic spam can effectively train classifiers to detect real spam. Table~\ref{tab:performance_summary} presents baseline performance (0\% synthetic) and degradation patterns for both LLM models using original prompt and cross-group mixing strategy.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Forward Detection Performance: Baseline vs. 100\% Synthetic Data (Cross-Group Strategy, SVM)}
\label{tab:performance_summary}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Model} & \textbf{Baseline (0\%)} & \textbf{100\% Synthetic} & \textbf{Degradation} \\
& \textbf{(Mean $\pm$ SD)} & \textbf{(Mean $\pm$ SD)} & \textbf{(Relative)} \\
\midrule
GPT-4.1-mini & 0.627 $\pm$ 0.026 & 0.476 $\pm$ 0.033 & -24.1\% \\
Claude-3.5-Haiku & 0.627 $\pm$ 0.026 & 0.353 $\pm$ 0.046 & -43.7\% \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
}
\end{table}

The results reveal critical patterns in LLM-generated synthetic spam effectiveness:

\textbf{Both models share identical baselines} (F1 = 0.627), confirming proper experimental controls and eliminating baseline variance as a confounding factor. This shared baseline reflects the pure real spam training condition (0\% synthetic) before any LLM-generated data is introduced.

\textbf{Substantial performance degradation} occurs when replacing real spam with synthetic data. At 100\% synthetic, GPT-4.1-mini degrades by 24.1\% (F1: 0.627 → 0.476), while Claude-3.5-Haiku exhibits catastrophic degradation of 43.7\% (F1: 0.627 → 0.353). This indicates that current LLM-generated synthetic spam, while semantically plausible, fails to fully capture the feature distributions necessary for effective classifier training.

\textbf{Model selection critically impacts degradation severity}. Claude-3.5-Haiku underperforms GPT-4.1-mini by 19.6 percentage points at 100\% synthetic (0.353 vs 0.476), demonstrating significant model-dependent variation in synthetic data quality. The nearly 2$\times$ difference in degradation rates (43.7\% vs 24.1\%) suggests GPT-4.1-mini generates synthetic spam with better distributional alignment to real spam patterns, though both fall substantially short of real data effectiveness.

\subsection{Performance Visualization Across Prompts and Models}

Figure~\ref{fig:forward_gpt} and Figure~\ref{fig:forward_claude} present comprehensive F1-score trajectories across all three prompt strategies (original, strong, weak) for GPT-4.1-mini and Claude-3.5-Haiku respectively, revealing distinct degradation patterns as synthetic proportion increases from 0\% to 100\% under cross-group mixing strategy.

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{../output/full_experiments/ceas08_gpt41mini/plots/combined/prompts_comparison_cross_group_performance_curves.png}
\caption{Forward Detection: GPT-4.1-mini Performance Across Prompts (Cross-Group Strategy, SVM). All three prompt strategies (original/strong/weak) produce nearly identical trajectories, declining from baseline F1 = 0.63 to 0.42-0.48 at 100\% synthetic. This prompt invariance indicates GPT-4.1-mini generates consistent synthetic spam quality regardless of prompt guidance intensity. The gradual, linear degradation pattern suggests predictable performance trade-offs when substituting real spam with GPT-generated synthetic data. Shaded regions represent $\pm$1 standard deviation across 20 replicate groups.}
\label{fig:forward_gpt}
\end{figure*}

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{../output/full_experiments/ceas08_claude35haiku/plots/combined/prompts_comparison_cross_group_performance_curves.png}
\caption{Forward Detection: Claude-3.5-Haiku Performance Across Prompts (Cross-Group Strategy, SVM). Original and strong prompts perform comparably to GPT-4.1-mini (declining to F1 $\approx$ 0.35-0.40 at 100\% synthetic), but weak prompt exhibits catastrophic collapse to near-random performance (F1 $\approx$ 0.20 at extreme synthetic proportions). This dramatic prompt sensitivity indicates Claude-3.5-Haiku requires careful prompt engineering to generate effective synthetic spam, with weak guidance producing spam that lacks critical discriminative features. Shaded regions represent $\pm$1 standard deviation.}
\label{fig:forward_claude}
\end{figure*}

The visualizations reveal several critical patterns:

\textbf{GPT-4.1-mini demonstrates prompt invariance} with negligible variation across all three prompt strategies, producing nearly identical degradation trajectories (F1: 0.63 → 0.42-0.48). This robustness simplifies deployment—practitioners can use any prompt strategy without performance penalties.

\textbf{Claude-3.5-Haiku shows severe prompt sensitivity}. Original and strong prompts perform comparably to GPT (F1 $\approx$ 0.35-0.40 at 100\% synthetic), but weak prompts catastrophically collapse to near-random performance (F1 $\approx$ 0.20), indicating that insufficient prompt guidance causes Claude to generate spam lacking critical discriminative features necessary for classifier training.

\textbf{All degradation patterns exhibit high linearity} ($R^2 > 0.95$), confirming predictable, monotonic performance decline without threshold effects or sudden transitions. This linearity enables reliable performance estimation at intermediate synthetic ratios through linear interpolation.

\subsection{Reverse Detection: Detecting AI-Powered Spam}

To quantify degradation rates, we conduct linear regression analysis modeling F1-score as a function of synthetic ratio ($\theta$). Table~\ref{tab:sensitivity_analysis} presents sensitivity metrics across all methods.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Performance Degradation Sensitivity (Within-Group Strategy)}
\label{tab:sensitivity_analysis}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{
\begin{tabular}{llcccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Classifier} & \textbf{Method} & \textbf{Slope} & \textbf{$R^2$} & \textbf{$p$-value} & \textbf{Total $\Delta$} \\
& & \textbf{($\beta_1$)} & & & \textbf{(0$\rightarrow$100\%)} \\
\midrule
\multirow{3}{*}{SVM} & GPT-4.1-mini & -0.00154 & 0.990 & $<$10$^{-9}$ & -0.151 \\
& Claude-3.5-Haiku & -0.00289 & 0.985 & $<$10$^{-9}$ & -0.289 \\
& SMOTE & -0.00117 & 0.969 & $<$10$^{-8}$ & -0.117 \\
\midrule
\multirow{3}{*}{Random Forest} & GPT-4.1-mini & -0.00013 & 0.723 & $<$10$^{-3}$ & -0.016 \\
& Claude-3.5-Haiku & -0.00012 & 0.689 & $<$10$^{-3}$ & -0.012 \\
& SMOTE & +0.00039 & 0.327 & $<$10$^{-2}$ & +0.039 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
}
\end{table}

The regression analysis quantifies the stark method-classifier interactions. For SVM, Claude-3.5-Haiku demonstrates the steepest degradation slope (-0.00289), nearly twice GPT-4.1-mini's rate (-0.00154) and 2.5 times SMOTE's rate (-0.00117). All SVM relationships exhibit high linearity ($R^2 > 0.96$) and strong statistical significance ($p < 10^{-8}$), indicating consistent, predictable degradation patterns without threshold effects. SMOTE's intermediate degradation rate confirms feature-space interpolation's advantage over text-space generation for margin-based classifiers.

For Random Forest, the pattern inverts dramatically: SMOTE exhibits \emph{positive} slope (+0.00039), indicating performance \emph{improvement} as synthetic proportion increases. This counterintuitive result suggests SMOTE-generated feature vectors enhance Random Forest's decision boundary optimization through increased training diversity within the convex hull of real data. However, the lower $R^2$ (0.327) indicates substantial non-linearity, with improvements occurring primarily at moderate synthetic ratios before plateauing. Both LLMs show near-zero degradation slopes (-0.00012 to -0.00013), with moderate $R^2$ (0.69-0.72) suggesting similar non-linear patterns potentially involving saturation effects or threshold phenomena at extreme ratios.


\subsection{Mixing Strategy Comparison: Within-Group vs. Cross-Group}

The systematic comparison of within-group and cross-group mixing strategies reveals method-dependent sensitivity to distribution matching. Table~\ref{tab:strategy_comparison} compares both strategies at 100\% synthetic proportion where differences are most pronounced.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Mixing Strategy Performance at 100\% Synthetic (Original Prompt, F1-Score)}
\label{tab:strategy_comparison}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{
\begin{tabular}{llcc}
\toprule
\textbf{Method} & \textbf{Classifier} & \textbf{Within-Group} & \textbf{Cross-Group} \\
\midrule
\multirow{2}{*}{GPT-4.1-mini} & SVM & 0.476 $\pm$ 0.038 & 0.476 $\pm$ 0.033 \\
& Random Forest & 0.777 $\pm$ 0.034 & 0.788 $\pm$ 0.032 \\
\midrule
\multirow{2}{*}{Claude-3.5-Haiku} & SVM & 0.338 $\pm$ 0.029 & 0.353 $\pm$ 0.046 \\
& Random Forest & 0.781 $\pm$ 0.098 & 0.796 $\pm$ 0.081 \\
\midrule
\multirow{2}{*}{SMOTE} & SVM & 0.506 $\pm$ 0.027 & 0.525 $\pm$ 0.029 \\
& Random Forest & 0.817 $\pm$ 0.061 & 0.859 $\pm$ 0.041 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
}
\end{table}

The mixing strategy comparison reveals three key patterns:

\textbf{LLM methods show classifier-dependent strategy effects}. For both GPT and Claude, Random Forest achieves slightly better performance with cross-group mixing (+0.011 to +0.015 F1), suggesting ensemble methods benefit from the additional diversity introduced by out-of-distribution synthetic samples. Conversely, SVM shows mixed patterns: GPT achieves identical performance across strategies (0.476 vs. 0.476), while Claude shows modest cross-group improvement (0.353 vs. 0.338, +0.015). These marginal differences with overlapping confidence intervals indicate strategy selection has minimal practical impact for LLM-based augmentation.

\textbf{SMOTE demonstrates pronounced cross-group advantages} across both classifiers. For SVM, cross-group SMOTE outperforms within-group by 0.019 F1 points (0.525 vs. 0.506, +3.8\%). For Random Forest, the cross-group advantage is more substantial: +0.042 F1 points (0.859 vs. 0.817, +5.1\%). This pattern suggests feature-space interpolation benefits significantly from cross-group diversity, possibly because SMOTE's convex-hull sampling naturally generalizes across feature distributions without semantic drift concerns that affect LLM-generated text. The larger Random Forest improvement indicates ensemble methods particularly benefit from the increased feature-space diversity provided by cross-group SMOTE sampling.

\textbf{Practical implications}: The near-equivalent performance across mixing strategies for LLM methods simplifies deployment—practitioners can use either within-group (simpler, matches target distribution) or cross-group (more generalizable, useful when target groups have insufficient samples) without significant performance penalties. SMOTE's strong preference for cross-group mixing, particularly with Random Forest (+5.1\%), suggests practitioners should prioritize cross-group diversity over precise distribution matching when using feature-space augmentation methods to maximize classification performance.

\subsection{LLM Prompt Strategy Effectiveness Analysis}

Our evaluation of three prompt strategies (original, strong, weak) reveals model-dependent sensitivity. Table~\ref{tab:prompt_comparison} shows results at 50\% synthetic proportion.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Prompt Strategy Comparison at 50\% Synthetic Ratio (Within-Group)}
\label{tab:prompt_comparison}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{
\begin{tabular}{llcc}
\toprule
\textbf{Model} & \textbf{Prompt} & \textbf{SVM F1} & \textbf{RF F1} \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{4}{c}{\textit{Baseline: 0.627 $\pm$ 0.026 (SVM), 0.793 $\pm$ 0.041 (RF)}} \\
\midrule
\multirow{3}{*}{GPT-4.1-mini} & Original & 0.565 $\pm$ 0.032 & 0.780 $\pm$ 0.040 \\
& Strong & 0.564 $\pm$ 0.031 & 0.782 $\pm$ 0.039 \\
& Weak & 0.562 $\pm$ 0.030 & 0.782 $\pm$ 0.038 \\
\midrule
\multirow{3}{*}{Claude-3.5-Haiku} & Original & 0.550 $\pm$ 0.027 & 0.779 $\pm$ 0.062 \\
& Strong & 0.543 $\pm$ 0.034 & 0.768 $\pm$ 0.076 \\
& Weak & 0.496 $\pm$ 0.045 & 0.752 $\pm$ 0.104 \\
