\multicolumn{2}{l}{GPT Mean $\pm$ SD:} & \multicolumn{2}{c}{-0.016 $\pm$ 0.020} & \multicolumn{2}{c}{4/20 improved} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
}
\end{table}

The results reveal dramatic variability: SMOTE exhibits 13 groups (65\%) with improvement and 7 groups (35\%) with degradation, with changes ranging from -22.6\% to +22.5\%. This contrasts sharply with GPT-4.1-mini, which shows only 4 groups (20\%) with improvement and consistent modest degradation across most groups.

\subsubsection{Variance Analysis}

The standard deviation of performance changes quantifies the stability differences:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{SMOTE variance}: $\pm$0.083 (extremely high)
    \item \textbf{GPT variance}: $\pm$0.020 (low)
    \item \textbf{Claude variance}: $\pm$0.024 (low)
    \item \textbf{Variance ratio}: SMOTE exhibits \textbf{4.2$\times$ higher variance} than GPT
\end{itemize}

This high variance indicates SMOTE's performance is highly sensitive to the relationship between training and test feature distributions. Groups with low baseline performance (e.g., Group 10: 0.719) tend to show large improvements (+22.5\%), suggesting SMOTE helps when real data coverage is insufficient. Conversely, groups with high baseline performance (e.g., Group 0: 0.816) tend to show degradation (-27.7\%), suggesting SMOTE may disrupt already-effective feature representations.

\subsubsection{Comparison with LLM Stability}

LLM methods demonstrate fundamentally different variance patterns:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Consistent degradation}: GPT and Claude show modest, predictable degradation across nearly all groups
    \item \textbf{Low variance}: Standard deviations 4-5$\times$ smaller than SMOTE
    \item \textbf{Predictable behavior}: Performance changes cluster tightly around mean values
    \item \textbf{No extreme outliers}: Maximum degradation for GPT is -4.7\% (vs -27.7\% for SMOTE)
\end{itemize}

This stability-vs-performance trade-off has important implications for practical deployment. SMOTE offers potential for substantial performance gains but with unpredictable group-specific outcomes, while LLM methods provide reliable, consistent behavior with modest degradation. The mechanisms underlying this distribution-dependent behavior and practical implications for method selection are discussed in Section~\ref{sec:discussion}.

\subsection{Cross-Model Reverse Detection Analysis}

To complement the forward detection experiments, we evaluate \textbf{reverse detection}—the ability to detect synthetic spam generated by one LLM using classifiers trained on synthetic spam from a different LLM. This 1-vs-Rest cross-model design assesses whether classifiers can generalize across different synthetic spam generation mechanisms.

\subsubsection{Experimental Design and Baseline Characteristics}

The reverse detection experiments employ an asymmetric training-testing paradigm:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{GPT-4.1-mini Testing}: Classifiers trained on real ham + Claude-3.5-Haiku synthetic spam, tested on GPT-4.1-mini synthetic spam detection
    \item \textbf{Claude-3.5-Haiku Testing}: Classifiers trained on real ham + GPT-4.1-mini synthetic spam, tested on Claude-3.5-Haiku synthetic spam detection
\end{itemize}

Critically, the test set comprises 100\% synthetic spam (from the target model) mixed with real ham, creating a fundamentally different detection challenge than forward experiments. The baseline condition (training ratio = 0\%, pure real spam) tests whether classifiers trained solely on real spam can detect LLM-generated synthetic spam, while higher training ratios (50\%, 100\%) test cross-model generalization.

Table~\ref{tab:reverse_detection_performance} presents cross-model reverse detection performance across all training ratios for the original prompt within-group strategy.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Cross-Model Reverse Detection Performance (Original Prompt, Within-Group)}
\label{tab:reverse_detection_performance}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{
\begin{tabular}{llcccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Testing} & \textbf{Training} & \textbf{Classifier} & \textbf{0\%} & \textbf{50\%} & \textbf{100\%} \\
\textbf{Model} & \textbf{Ratio} & & \textbf{F1} & \textbf{F1} & \textbf{F1} \\
\midrule
\multirow{2}{*}{GPT-4.1-mini} & \multirow{2}{*}{(trained on Claude)} & SVM & 0.312 $\pm$ 0.068 & 0.538 $\pm$ 0.053 & 0.487 $\pm$ 0.067 \\
& & Random Forest & 0.829 $\pm$ 0.028 & 0.832 $\pm$ 0.034 & 0.882 $\pm$ 0.053 \\
\midrule
\multirow{2}{*}{Claude-3.5-Haiku} & \multirow{2}{*}{(trained on GPT)} & SVM & 0.078 $\pm$ 0.053 & 0.195 $\pm$ 0.079 & 0.255 $\pm$ 0.071 \\
& & Random Forest & 0.812 $\pm$ 0.022 & 0.813 $\pm$ 0.030 & 0.817 $\pm$ 0.033 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
}
\end{table}

The results reveal fundamental asymmetries in cross-model detectability:

\textbf{GPT synthetic spam exhibits higher baseline detectability}: Classifiers trained solely on real spam achieve F1 = 0.312 (SVM) and 0.829 (Random Forest) when detecting GPT synthetic spam, substantially outperforming Claude detection (F1 = 0.078 SVM, 0.812 RF). This suggests GPT's synthetic spam retains stronger distributional similarities to real spam patterns, enabling non-trivial detection even without cross-model training data.

\textbf{Cross-model training provides differential benefits}: For GPT testing, incorporating Claude synthetic training data improves SVM performance dramatically (0.312 → 0.538 at 50\%, +72.4\%), though 100\% synthetic shows slight degradation (0.487). For Claude testing, cross-model training yields more modest improvements (SVM: 0.078 → 0.255 at 100\%, +227\% relative but only +0.177 absolute), indicating GPT training data provides limited transferable features for detecting Claude spam.

\textbf{Random Forest demonstrates remarkable cross-model robustness}: Both testing scenarios achieve F1 $\approx$ 0.81-0.88 across all training ratios, with GPT testing showing slight improvement at 100\% synthetic (0.882). This consistency indicates ensemble methods effectively extract cross-model invariant features, maintaining high detection accuracy regardless of which LLM generated the training vs testing synthetic spam.

\textbf{SVM exhibits severe cross-model transfer limitations}: Maximum SVM performance remains moderate even with 100\% cross-model synthetic training (GPT: 0.487, Claude: 0.255), dramatically lower than forward detection baselines (0.627). This suggests margin-based classifiers struggle to learn decision boundaries that generalize across different LLM generation mechanisms, while Random Forest's ensemble voting naturally aggregates diverse patterns.

\subsubsection{Comparison with Forward Detection}

The cross-model reverse detection results contrast sharply with forward detection patterns, revealing distinct challenges:

\textbf{Inverted performance baselines}: Forward detection baselines (pure real training, real test) achieve F1 = 0.627 (SVM) and 0.793 (RF). Reverse detection baselines (pure real training, synthetic test) drop to F1 = 0.078-0.312 (SVM) and 0.812-0.829 (RF), indicating synthetic spam detection requires synthetic training examples for SVM but not RF.

\textbf{Training ratio effects reverse}: In forward detection, increasing synthetic ratio degrades performance (especially for SVM: -24.1\% to -46.0\% at 100\%). In reverse detection, increasing cross-model synthetic ratio \emph{improves} SVM performance (+72.4\% to +227\%), as cross-model synthetic data provides critical features for detecting the target model's spam.

\textbf{Architectural advantages persist}: Random Forest maintains superior and stable performance across both paradigms (forward: 0.77-0.79, reverse: 0.81-0.88), while SVM shows extreme context dependence (forward: 0.34-0.63, reverse: 0.08-0.54). This robustness makes ensemble methods preferable for both generation quality assessment (forward) and cross-model spam detection (reverse).

